AT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift and TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n\nE006 priority note: per the docs-authoring priority exception, E006's priority\nis derived from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0 (HLR-6\nitself is P2; the exception deliberately decouples docs priority from the HLR's\nlevel and from priority inheritance).\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001 --> E002\n    E001 --> E003\n    E001 --> E006\n    E002 --> E006\n    E003 --> E006\n    E004 --> E006\n    E005 --> E006\n```\n\nE004 and E005 are roots (no dependencies); E001 is a root; E006 is the sole\nsink. Critical path: E001 → E002 → E006 (length 3).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Investigation edge (Rule 3) | The assessment (HLR-1) produces the definitive GA scope; the size and content of the API-stability/hardening work is unknown until it lands (strategy Open Question 1). True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E003 | Investigation edge (Rule 3) | Same assessment gates the support-posture and hardening scope (scale limits, error handling gap remediation). True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) + content dependency | GA documentation and TP→GA migration notes must reflect the definitive GA scope the assessment produces. |\n| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics — docs describe what was built. |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| E002 ∥ E003 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repo/component, different areas (API versioning & upgrade testing vs. support policy & configuration matrix); no shared build-against artifact. Merge-conflict risk is coordination, not dependency. |\n| E001 ∥ E004, E005 (no edge) | Rule 3 (decision rule) | HLR-4 and HLR-5 are fully specified by their HLRs; the assessment's outcome does not change whether these epics exist or what they deliver, so they are not gated. |\n| E004 ∥ E005 (no edge) | Rule 14 | Different components, different repos, no shared artifacts. |\n| No konflux/onboarding edges | Rule 10 / Step 2 | Llama Stack, TrustyAI, and Dashboard are all in the platform and Konflux-onboarded per architecture context; no onboarding epics exist, so Rules 5–10 ordering constraints do not apply. |\n\nRule 19 (safety-critical strategies) was evaluated and deliberately not\ntriggered: the strategy does not introduce new guardrail, sandboxing, or RBAC\nmechanisms — HLR-4 promotes an already-shipped TrustyAI guardrails integration\nfrom an experimental flag to a supported, documented integration. Fail-mode\nand security validation are folded into E004 as acceptance criteria (defined\nbehavior when the guardrails provider is unavailable; validation on a\nGA-configuration distribution).\n\nThe assessment's outcome is unbounded (an inventory of unknown gaps, not a\nchoice among ≤3 bounded options), so no conditional branch epics are\ngenerated; the gate is modeled as a blocking edge with `rewrite` impact. If\nthe assessment surfaces hardening work that fits neither the API-stability\nepic nor the support-posture epic, that work becomes follow-up scope per the\nassessment's own recommendation (noted in Health Warnings).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | GA-readiness assessment producing the definitive GA scope. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases. |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | E0