ustyAI deployed, the\n  guardrails integration works without any experimental opt-in flag, and a\n  request subject to a configured guardrail is handled per that guardrail.\n- Fail-mode behavior is defined, implemented, and verified: with the\n  guardrails provider unavailable, the distribution behaves per the documented\n  policy (e.g., fail closed or open per configuration) — not with an undefined\n  error.\n- The integration is documented end-to-end (configuration, setup, behavior,\n  fail-mode), and the documentation matches the validated behavior.\n- The experimental flag (or its equivalent gating) is removed or retired such\n  that no user action is required to reach the supported configuration.\n- Misconfiguration produces an actionable error identifying the misconfigured\n  resource.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-4 (P1): safety guardrails via TrustyAI are a supported, documented\n  integration (not an experimental flag) for GA distributions. This epic\n  covers that requirement in full.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":36322,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd47e305685c09a4","agent_id":"a0c39dc715a16ddec","session_id":"e9571ff2-2ec2-4b20-9ef4-1431f27af9f3","uuid":"cf6defd8-b3b5-4421-af66-664aa9939922","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:22:54.798Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9014"}
