osition, and no design question affects the\n126\tdocumentation approach. E003's `human_process_gates: -1` (SLO/backport\n127\tcommitments need sign-off) vs E002's `0` is an arguable but reasonable line.\n128\t\n129\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n130\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from HLR text. Rule-mandated ACs present:\n131\tE005 (a replacement of the opt-in behavior) carries a rollback AC; E006\n132\t(docs-authoring) carries the technical-review AC; no konflux-chain epics\n133\texist, so no build-pipeline AC applies. No AC states an externally-controlled\n134\tschedule or availability precondition — E002/E003's \"deferral accepted by the\n135\tstrategy owner\" clauses are governance of the epic's own deliverable, and\n136\tE003's exclusion-with-rationale is the epic's own decision output. No epic\n137\tbody references sibling epics by draft ID (cross-epic references use names\n138\tlike \"the support-posture work\" and \"the readiness assessment\").\n139\t\n140\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n141\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: each HLR's capabilities appear in the\n142\tcorresponding epic's scope and ACs; the problem statement's gap dimensions\n143\t(API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) are all carried\n144\tin E001's scope and remediated via E002/E003; the out-of-scope boundaries are\n145\trespected (no new feature development, no deprecation of alternative serving\n146\tpaths). The strategy's single open question is carried forward as a\n147\tdocumented gate (E001 with rewrite impact) and a Health Warning, not silently\n148\tdropped. No conditional branches are needed (the assessment's outcome is an\n149\tunbounded inventory, not a choice among bounded options — correctly reasoned).\n150\tThe docs-authoring obligation is met by E006 with Rule 11 ordering.\n151\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a90e7f849c2e91d1","agent_id":"a21597dba666eb135","session_id":"e9571ff2-2ec2-4b20-9ef4-1431f27af9f3","uuid":"3ab85582