{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3ca18b0f3f4f8f9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9014-E004\n3\ttitle: TrustyAI guardrails as supported Llama Stack integration\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n5\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n6\tteam: TrustyAI\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 0\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 0\n17\t  human_process_gates: -1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 4\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# TrustyAI guardrails as supported Llama Stack integration\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic promotes the TrustyAI safety-guardrails integration for Llama Stack\n34\tdistributions from an experimental flag to a supported, documented\n35\tintegration, as GA distributions require. The integration already exists\n36\tbehind an experimental opt-in; this epic makes it a first-class supported\n37\tsurface: validated on GA configurations, safe in its failure modes, and\n38\tdocumented for users. The work spans the TrustyAI operator/integration side\n39\tand the Llama Stack distribution configuration that consumes it; the TrustyAI\n40\tteam owns the integration's support posture.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Remove the experimental gating so the TrustyAI guardrails integration is a\n45\t  supported configuration for GA Llama Stack distributions (not an\n46\t  experimental flag).\n47\t- Validate the integration end-to-end on a GA-configuration distribution:\n48\t  guardrails requests flow between the Llama Stack distribution and the\n49\t  TrustyAI service and are enforced as configured.\n50\t- Define and validate fail-mode behavior: what happens to requests and to the\n51\t  distribution when the guardrails provider is unavailable or misconfigured,\n52\t  with the degradation explicit rather than emergent.\n53\t- Document the supported integration: supported configuration, setup, and\n54\t  behavior, including the fail-mode behavior above, as user-facing\n55\t  documentation for the integration.\n56\t- Confirm the supported designation with the support posture it implies\n57\t  (where users report issues, what is covered).\n58\t\n59\t## Acceptance Criteria\n60\t\n61\t- On a GA-configuration Llama Stack distribution with TrustyAI deployed, the\n62\t  guardrails integration works without any experimental opt-in flag, and a\n63\t  request subject to a configured guardrail is handled per that guardrail.\n64\t- Fail-mode behavior is defined, implemented, and verified: with the\n65\t  guardrails provider unavailable, the distribution behaves per the documented\n66\t  policy (e.g., fail closed or open per configuration) — not with an undefined\n67\t  error.\n68\t- The integration is documented end-to-end (configuration, setup, behavior,\n69\t  fail-mode), and the documentation matches the validated behavior.\n70\t- The experimental flag (or its equivalent gating) is removed or retired such\n71\t  that no user action is required to reach the supported configuration.\n72\t- Misconfiguration produces an actionable error identifying the misconfigured\n73\t  resource.\n74\t\n75\t## HLR Traceability\n76\t\n77\t- HLR-4 (P1): safety guardrails via TrustyAI are a supported, documented\n78\t  integration (not an experimental flag) for GA distributions. This epic\n79\t  covers that requirement in full.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a90e7f849c2e91d1","agent_id":"a21597dba666eb135","session_id":"e9571ff2-2ec2-4b20-9ef4-1431f27af9f3","uuid":"c05ff16a-cdd8-40ca-840b-c3b06cf023e8","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:28:15.700Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3ca